+# Architecture Plan: Maintenance Mode
+
+## Problem
+
+Individual route handlers/controllers and WebSocket message handlers need to be markable
+as "affected by maintenance mode". A runtime-toggleable flag (no redeploy) determines
+whether marked handlers currently reject calls before their logic runs. Unmarked handlers
+must be unaffected. One mechanism must cover both HTTP and WS.
+
+## Key finding driving the design
+
+Guards (`CanActivate` + `ExecutionContext`) are already the single mechanism Nest uses to
+gate handler execution for **both** transports, ahead of the handler body:
+
+- HTTP: `packages/core/router` (`RouterExecutionContext`) resolves guards via
+  `core/guards` (`GuardsContextCreator`) and evaluates them via `GuardsConsumer.tryActivate`
+  before invoking the handler.
+- WS: `packages/websockets/context/ws-context-creator.ts` (`WsContextCreator`) resolves
+  guards through the *same* `core/guards` classes and calls
+  `fnCanActivate(args)` — which wraps `GuardsConsumer.tryActivate` — before
+  `interceptorsConsumer.intercept(...)` runs the message handler.
+
+`GuardsConsumer.tryActivate` calls `guard.canActivate(context)` with no try/catch, so an
+exception thrown by a guard propagates to the caller unmodified. Each transport already
+has its own exception-handling layer downstream (HTTP exception filters / WS
+`exceptionHandler` in `wsProxy.create`), so a guard that **throws** an `HttpException`
+subclass is reported correctly on both transports without either cell needing to know
+about maintenance mode.
+
+Consequence: maintenance mode needs no changes to `core/router`, `core/guards`, or
+`packages/websockets/context` — it is implemented entirely as a new `CanActivate`
+guard plus a decorator, consumed the same way any other guard already is
+(`@UseGuards(...)` per-handler/class, or `APP_GUARD` globally).
+
+## Dependency direction constraint
+
+`packages/common` has zero dependency on `packages/core` (verified via `package.json`);
+`packages/core` depends on `@nestjs/common`. `Reflector` lives in `packages/core/services`.
+Therefore the metadata-setting decorator (no `Reflector` needed — same `SetMetadata`-style
+pattern as `packages/common/decorators/core/use-guards.decorator.ts`) must live in
+`packages/common`, while the guard (needs `Reflector`) must live in `packages/core`.
+
+## New cells
+
+### 1. `packages/common/maintenance` (leaf — no cell dependencies)
+
+```yaml
+"Maintainable() -> decorator: CustomDecorator<string>":
+  location: maintainable.decorator.ts
+  # Routine. Method/class decorator, same construction pattern as SetMetadata:
+  # Reflect.defineMetadata on descriptor.value (method) or target (class).
+  # Exposes its metadata key as `.KEY` (CustomDecorator convention already
+  # used by SetMetadata in this codebase) so packages/core/maintenance can
+  # read it via Reflector without duplicating the key string.
+
+"MaintenanceModeState()":
+  location: maintenance-mode-state.ts
+  # Entity. @Injectable(). In-memory toggle, starts disabled.
+  methods:
+    "enable()": turn maintenance mode on
+    "disable()": turn maintenance mode off
+    "isEnabled() -> enabled:boolean": current state
+```
+
+No `Imports` — mirrors `packages/core/adapters` as the other zero-dependency cell in
+this project.
+
+### 2. `packages/core/maintenance` (depends on `common/maintenance`, `common/exceptions`)
+
+```yaml
+Imports:
+  - Types:
+      - Maintainable
+      - MaintenanceModeState
+    From: packages/common/maintenance
+  - Types:
+      - ServiceUnavailableException
+    From: packages/common/exceptions
+
+"MaintenanceGuard(state: MaintenanceModeState)":
+  location: maintenance.guard.ts
+  # Entity implementing CanActivate (ambient common/interfaces type, same
+  # convention as core/guards: not tracked via formal Imports).
+  # Constructor also takes a core-native Reflector (packages/core/services,
+  # undocumented cell, same precedent as core/guards' own dependencies).
+  methods:
+    "canActivate(context: ExecutionContext) -> allowed:boolean":
+      Algorithm:
+      1. reflector.getAllAndOverride(Maintainable.KEY, [context.getHandler(), context.getClass()])
+      2. Not marked -> return true
+      3. Marked and state.isEnabled() is false -> return true
+      4. Marked and state.isEnabled() is true -> throw ServiceUnavailableException
+```
+
+Consumers apply `@Maintainable()` to a method or controller class, and register
+`MaintenanceGuard` the normal way (`@UseGuards(MaintenanceGuard)` locally, or
+`{ provide: APP_GUARD, useClass: MaintenanceGuard }` globally — global registration
+means the guard runs for every handler but only ever denies the ones carrying
+`@Maintainable()`). Same decorator, same guard class, same registration story for
+HTTP controllers and WS gateways.
+
+## Unit testability
+
+`MaintenanceGuard` is a plain class (`new MaintenanceGuard(new MaintenanceModeState())`
+plus a `Reflector`). Tests decorate a throwaway class/method with `@Maintainable()`,
+build a minimal fake `ExecutionContext` (`getHandler`/`getClass` stubs — no HTTP
+server, no socket), toggle `state.enable()/disable()`, and assert `canActivate()`
+rejects only for the marked target. This is the same test shape `core/guards` already
+uses for `GuardsConsumer`/`GuardsContextCreator`.
+
+## Dependency diagram
+
+```
+packages/common/maintenance   packages/common/exceptions
+        |                              |
+        +---------------+--------------+
+                         |
+              packages/core/maintenance
+                         |
+        (consumed like any CanActivate: @UseGuards / APP_GUARD)
+                         |
+        +----------------+----------------+
+        |                                 |
+packages/core/router              packages/websockets/context
+(HTTP, unchanged)                 (WS, unchanged)
+```
